# Finite integrated Backup and TAP collector review

This folder preserves the production Backup source and the accepted staged source identities read during an independent integration review. No production, shared, or Automation source was edited. No Backup behavioral suite, original restic command, timing comparison, or memory comparison was rerun. The existing final parent-owned 47-test receipt is preserved with its exact collector, verifier, source-before record and raw streams; its SHA-256 is `a0e8198e438fb9c5cc08d0394793bc684682efa82fde7c4bca0ca202038bfec0`.

The 34 accepted staged fingerprints were verified. Rabin, capture, crypto, KDF and shared storage match their accepted bytes. Production restic differs only in the capture import. Compression differs only in its preserved-source location comment. Test import locations and the original executable/archive relative locations resolve and authenticate correctly. Fixture configuration edits affect only synthetic owned repositories. Child-local `fs`/clock hooks restore in `finally`; the snapshot publication/cleanup/deadline tests preserve their accepted behavior contracts. No additional implementation integration defect was confirmed.

The observed collector defect is separate from Backup behavior. The exact baseline decoder joins bounded contiguous TAP comment fragments and reverses doubled backslashes, but Node v24.16.0 also escapes `#` as `\#`. A single synthetic JSON logger test passed while that decoder rejected a Windows path containing `#`. The failed observation, raw TAP, old module and Node's built-in `tapEscape` source are retained. `independent-decoder.mjs` is the proposed bounded one-pass replacement, with the root-compatible `readRabinObservation` export. It reverses `\\` and `\#` without introducing a second replacement layer, requires one intact marker prefix, rejects duplicate markers, and retains the 4096-line/1MiB text bound. The bound counts UTF-16 code units, not process RSS. The actual synthetic logger includes a 90,000-character message, Windows paths, literal backslash followed by `#`, Unicode, escaped controls, quotes and newlines. Every content split position in a smaller escaped JSON message is checked, including splits between escape bytes.

All four markers reconstruct from each of the retained first, second and final 47-test streams: 30 file-boundary comparisons, 30 boundary command observations, 20 lifecycle cases, three deadline cases and four snapshot command observations. These counts describe retained parent executions. They are not new original-reader executions by this reviewer. The independent collector tests cover reconstruction, exact values and rejection of truncation, interrupted/non-comment fragments, missing or duplicate markers and excessive input.

Fixture cleanup checks the owned lexical temporary parent and prefix. It is not atomic isolation from a hostile external process; a setup failure before an after-hook is installed can leave an owned synthetic folder. Capture's source-change guards and post-await deadline checks cannot cancel an already pending kernel operation or establish an atomic filesystem snapshot. Failed snapshots may leave authenticated immutable unindexed packs. Real kernel close failures and all adversarial filesystem races are not comprehensively covered. These limits remain explicit; this review makes no full restic parity, original-grade security or performance superiority claim.

Two mechanical review-script corrections are also preserved: the first comparison initially assumed compression's source-reference comment matched byte-for-byte, and the import inventory initially named the original test with an incorrect filename. Neither was a production failure. The exact failed synthetic decoder probe remains the confirmed recorder finding.
